1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,000
 Hello everyone, today I would like to talk to you about a very concrete topic, how artificial intelligence begins to be integrated in our daily life as well as in our professional life as personal.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:16,000
 When we talk about artificial intelligence, we immediately think about Chajipiti, Claude, Algemini and even Mistral.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:22,000
 However, these last tools, however, behind these tools, there are very different technologies and uses.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:31,000
 For example, a large language model, which is often called LLM, can be understood with a written letter, analyzed a document or helped us to produce content.

5
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:37,000
 But you have to keep in mind one thing, the model does not understand the world as a human being.

6
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
 It works from the context of a prompt, of the information that we provide it with.

7
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
 In my development projects, I use, notably, Chajipiti, Claude Code and Cursor.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:51,000
 Claude Code can work directly in a project, read the files, modify the code, execute some commands.

9
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,000
 Cursor, on its side, is an environment of development that allows us to use artificial intelligence directly in the editor.

10
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:02,000
 But this is an important question. Until when should we leave artificial intelligence to act on its own?

11
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,000
 Personally, I prefer to keep a human control over the important decision.

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:13,000
 The artificial intelligence can be proposed, analyzed and executed in certain actions, but the final decision must remain understandable and verifiable.

13
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:24,000
 Let's take an example. Let's imagine that I develop a macOS application called STIA. This goal is very simple. I upload a video, the application extracts the audio track, publish it,

14
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
 it automatically generates a file with the name SRT, as well as a transcription in the format text.

15
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
 Everything must be treated locally on my Macbook Pro, Equipening Pulse, Apple, Silicon, M4.

16
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:41,000
 The video must never be sent with Veropen I, Anthropik or another distant call.

17
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
 To implement this application, we can use Tori, React, TypeScript or the rest.

18
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:53,000
 FFMpeg is responsible for preparing the audio, then Whisper.cpp makes the transcription directly on the machine.

19
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:59,000
 Then, the SRT file can be imported into Davichi Resolve to add subtitles to the video.

20
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:02,000
 This type of architecture also poses a question of performance.

21
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,000
 Does this happen with a video of 10 minutes, 30 minutes or even an hour?

22
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:10,000
 How many memory does it take to use it? How much time does it take to load the model?

23
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,000
 Can the result remain reliable when we pronounce the technical terms,

24
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,000
 non-proper or non-English instead of a French sentence?

25
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,000
 Let's take a few examples.

26
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:25,000
 OpenI, ChargeGPT, Anthropik, Cloudcode, Cursor, Kit, Whisper, FFMpeg, Davichi Resolve,

27
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
 Apple, Silicon, API, LLM, Prompt, TypeScript and Rust.

28
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:34,000
 And now a few numbers. The video has been recorded on August 7, 2026.

29
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,000
 It lasts about 3 minutes.

30
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
 The machine has 16 giga-octets of live memory.

31
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:46,000
 A model can weigh 747 mega-octets while another is over 5.4 giga-octets.

32
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,000
 Finally, one last question.

33
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,000
 Can a local local artificial intelligence really become a little simple,

34
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,000
 fast and reliable, without sending our data to the cloud?

35
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,000
 It is precisely what we are going to try to discover with STI.

36
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
 Thank you for watching this video and see you very soon!

